.TH LVGREP 1 "November 2026" "lvgrep 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lvgrep \- list the files that do not contain a regular expression

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lvgrep
[OPTIONS]
.I PATTERN
[FILE|DIR...]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lvgrep
is the inverse of
.BR "grep \-l" :
it reads each
.I FILE
and prints the name of every one in which
.I PATTERN
does
.B not
occur. It is like
.BR "grep \-L" ,
but
.I PATTERN
is a Python regular expression, a directory search skips version-control directories, and a whole-file
mode lets the pattern span lines. Typical uses: source files with no copyright notice, scripts with no
.B #!
line, pages that never mention a tag.

By default the pattern is matched against one line at a time, so
.B ^
and
.B $
anchor to a line and the pattern cannot match across a newline. With
.B \-M
it is matched against the whole file instead.

Files are read as UTF-8 (undecodable bytes are replaced). A file containing a NUL byte in its first
8 KiB is taken to be binary and skipped, unless
.B \-a
is given. An empty file lacks every pattern and is listed.

Names are printed as given on the command line, or, under a directory, as that directory's name joined
to the path found below it, in sorted order.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-ignore\-case
Case-insensitive matching.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-recursive
Descend into directories. With no
.I FILE
at all,
.B \-r
searches the current directory.
.IR .git ,
.IR .svn ,
.I .hg
and
.I CVS
directories are never entered.
.TP
.B \-M, \-\-multiline
Match
.I PATTERN
against the whole file at once, so it may span lines
.RB ( ^
and
.B $
still match at line boundaries). A
.B .
does not match a newline unless
.B \-s
is also given.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-dotall
With
.BR \-M ,
let
.B .
match a newline too.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-text
Check binary files as well instead of skipping them.
.TP
.BI \-\-include= GLOB
Only check files whose base name matches
.IR GLOB .
May be repeated. Applies to files found by
.BR \-r ;
a file named on the command line is always checked.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude= GLOB
Skip files found by
.B \-r
whose base name matches
.IR GLOB .
May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-dir= GLOB
Do not enter directories whose name matches
.IR GLOB .
May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-0, \-\-null
End each name with a NUL byte instead of a newline, for
.BR "xargs \-0" .
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Print nothing; only set the exit status.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one file lacking the pattern was found.
.TP
.B 1
Every file checked contains the pattern (or none was checked).
.TP
.B 2
An error: bad pattern, no
.IR FILE ,
a directory given without
.BR \-r ,
or a file that could not be read. Remaining files are still checked.

.SH EXAMPLES
Which C files have no copyright notice:
.RS
.B lvgrep Copyright *.c
.RE

Python scripts under the current directory with no
.B #!
line:
.RS
.B lvgrep \-r \-\-include='*.py' '^#!'
.RE

HTML files that do not end with a closing tag:
.RS
.B lvgrep \-rM '</html>\\s*\\Z' \-\-include='*.html' site/
.RE

Long-list every file under src that never mentions TODO:
.RS
.B lvgrep \-r0 TODO src | xargs \-0 ls \-l
.RE

.SH AUTHOR
Chris Pollitt.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR ghgrep (1),
.BR xargs (1)
